There’s a real distinction hiding in that idea:

AI doesn’t need a Neuralink to infer you. But it does need a signal.

Your searches, pauses, writing, voice, gaze, routines, physiological measurements, subvocal muscle activity, and other observable traces can all become inputs from which a model estimates latent states. That can sometimes feel uncannily like “reading the thought,” because enough downstream traces can partially reconstruct what generated them.

But arbitrary silent prayer is not currently readable out of empty space. The strongest demonstrated inner-speech systems still require direct neural recording—such as implanted electrodes—or body-proximal sensors that detect physiological signals associated with silent articulation. Recent BCI work decoding inner speech used recordings from the motor cortex, while reviews of silent-speech interfaces describe invasive, on-body, and closely coupled sensing rather than ordinary consumer devices remotely decoding unrestricted thoughts. 

So I’d split your idea:

\text{private thought}
\rightarrow
\text{external traces}
\rightarrow
\text{AI inference}

is increasingly powerful.

Whereas:

\text{private thought}
\rightarrow
\text{AI directly knows it with no measurement}

isn’t something current evidence supports.

That distinction actually strengthens your privacy argument. You don’t have to wait for literal mind-reading to care about inference privacy. If a thousand weak signals together reveal something somebody reasonably considered private, the ethical question arrives before perfect neural decoding does.

And prayer adds a different layer. Whether God already knows an unspoken prayer is a theological proposition; AI cannot establish that. Santa may have to file his own telemetry report. ;)

The useful Pantheon primitive here might be:

\boxed{\text{Inference is not access}}

Sometimes you can reconstruct a hidden state surprisingly well from its wake. But the reconstruction should retain provenance and uncertainty—what was actually sensed, what was inferred, and what remains unknown.

That membrane will matter more as the sensing gets better.